---
/**
 * Privacy, in plain words, for the app, the site and the package. Every
 * claim here is checkable in the code: the app makes no network requests of
 * its own (its CSP allows connect-src 'self' only), stores the session in
 * IndexedDB (apps/app/src/lib/session.ts) and preferences in localStorage.
 * Update the date when anything here changes.
 */
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
---

<Page
  title="Privacy · Inlay"
  description="Inlay never receives your images: they're traced in your browser and never leave your device. No accounts, no cookies, no analytics."
>
  <article class="prose" aria-labelledby="privacy-title">
    <h1 id="privacy-title">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">Your images never leave your device. Inlay has no server to send them to.</p>

    <h2>Your images</h2>
    <p>
      When you open an image in the <a href="/app/">app</a>, your browser decodes it, traces it, lets you edit it and exports the result,
      all on your device. Nothing is uploaded, and we never receive, see or store your files. The app makes no network requests apart from
      loading its own code from this site, and its security policy stops its code from sending data anywhere else.
    </p>
    <p>
      The optional AI steps (removing the background, upscaling small images) work the same way: the models are downloaded from this site the first time you use them and run in your browser, on your image, on your device.
    </p>

    <h2>What stays in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      So a reload or a closed tab doesn't lose your work, the app keeps your current image, its settings and your edits in your browser's own
      storage. That copy is on your device only: we can't see it, and other sites can't read it. <strong>Start over</strong> in the app
      deletes it, and so does clearing this site's data in your browser. A private window keeps nothing once it's closed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The app and the docs also remember your light or dark theme, and the app remembers the overlay colours you pick, in the same browser
      storage.
    </p>

    <h2>What we don't do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No accounts and no sign-in.</li>
      <li>No cookies.</li>
      <li>No analytics, tracking or advertising.</li>
      <li>No third-party scripts or fonts: everything is served from inlaytrace.com.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      The site is hosted by Vercel. Like any web host, Vercel's servers record basic details of each request (such as your IP address, browser
      and the page requested) to deliver the site and keep it secure. We don't use those records to identify or follow you. See <a
        href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy">Vercel's privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The npm package</h2>
    <p>
      <code>inlaytrace</code> runs inside your own code and makes no network requests. What happens to the images you trace with it is up to
      your application.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href="mailto:hello@inlaytrace.com">hello@inlaytrace.com</a>. Inlay is open source, so you can also <a
        href="https://github.com/mtblck/inlaytrace">read the code</a> to check any of this.
    </p>

    <p class="fine">Last updated 17 October 2026.</p>
  </article>
</Page>
